{
    "background_story": "Presley was a young and talented sculptor who had always been fascinated by the works of the ancient Greek master, Lysippus. Growing up, he spent hours studying the few remaining pieces of Lysippus' art, marveling at the way he was able to capture the subtleties of human emotion in bronze. Presley became determined to follow in Lysippus' footsteps, honing his own skills and developing a unique style that blended traditional techniques with modern sensibilities. After years of apprenticeships and solo exhibitions, Presley's big break came when he was commissioned to create a large-scale public installation inspired by Lysippus' iconic 'Alexander the Great'. The piece, titled 'Ephemeral Hero', was met with critical acclaim and cemented Presley's reputation as a leading figure in the art world. He continued to draw inspiration from Lysippus, pushing the boundaries of sculpture and exploring new ways to capture the human experience.",
